# User Data Directories
export/
backup/
# Any manual safety copy of an export taken before a run. The bare `export/`
# rule above does not cover a differently-named sibling, and these hold
# gigabytes of the user's own photographs -- a single `git add -A` would
# otherwise stage the entire library.
export-backup/
export-*/
# Likewise for alternate archive destinations: `--backup-dir backup-test` and
# friends are how a run is verified without disturbing the existing archive, and
# each one holds gigabytes of the user's own photographs.
backup-*/

# Photographs and video, ANYWHERE in the tree -- not just in the directories
# above. This repo is public and its subject is a personal photo library, so it
# sits one `git add -A` away from publishing family photos. The path rules above
# only help for files that stay inside those directories; a stray photo at the
# repo root or an accidental fixture under tests/ is staged normally without
# these.
#
# This is the second of three layers, and the weakest of them: it matches by
# NAME, so a real JPEG called `notes.txt` slips straight through. The
# content-inspecting pre-commit hook in .githooks/ is what catches that case --
# enable it once per clone with `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`.
#
# Tests never need these rules relaxed: every fixture is generated at runtime
# into a tempfile.mkdtemp() sandbox, never committed.
*.heic
*.HEIC
*.heif
*.HEIF
*.jpg
*.JPG
*.jpeg
*.JPEG
*.png
*.PNG
*.gif
*.GIF
*.tif
*.TIF
*.tiff
*.TIFF
*.bmp
*.webp
*.avif
*.dng
*.DNG
*.raw
*.cr2
*.nef
*.arw
*.mov
*.MOV
*.mp4
*.MP4
*.m4v
*.M4V
*.avi
*.mkv
*.webm
*.3gp
# Apple edit-history sidecars carry no image data, but they belong to the same
# personal library and have no business in version control either.
*.aae
*.AAE

